en_desc:
The pure land brought about by the power of Amitābha Buddha's vows, where those reborn do not fall back and attain Buddhahood. “Sukhāvatī” means “possessing bliss”; the sūtras describe the sentient beings of that land as having none of the many sufferings but experiencing only various pleasures, and the core of “having none of the many sufferings” lies in “non-retrogression” — those reborn no longer fall back.

The nature of Sukhāvatī is understood differently among the schools:
- The [[pureland|Pure Land]] school (Shandao) judges it a “reward land,” which ordinary beings can enter by [[two_paths_two_powers|other-power]];
- Yogācāra and some Tiantai commentators judge it a “transformation land,” manifested in response to capacities;
- Tiantai has in addition the doctrine of the “four lands” (the land of co-residence of ordinary beings and sages, the land of expedient remainder, the land of true reward without obstruction, and the land of eternal quiescent light), and judges Sukhāvatī to be, for instance, the land of co-residence of ordinary beings and sages.

These differences do not affect the practical core of the Pure Land school (faith, vows, and recitation of the name), but they do affect the explanation of the nature of “rebirth” — whether one is born into a real land adorned by true reward or enters a provisional land of responsive transformation.

The four classes into which the tantras are divided according to their mode of practice and their aim:

- **Kriya-tantra** - chiefly external purification, offering and recitation of mantras, with the deity as a guest;
- **Carya-tantra** - external acts and [[southern_tradition|inner contemplation]] in equal measure;
- **Yoga-tantra** - stresses inner samadhi and visualisation, with the deity as a friend;
- **Anuttarayoga-tantra** - the highest level, in which the deity is oneself ([[sokushin_jobutsu|attaining Buddhahood in this very body]]); in Tibetan Buddhism it is further divided into father tantras, mother tantras and non-dual tantras.

The point of the classification is to determine **the stages of practice and the precepts**: each of the four classes has its own initiations, samaya precepts, mandalas and rituals, and practice in a higher class must rest on the ethical basis of the lower. The 'four classes' are therefore not merely a ranking by difficulty but a complete system of training.

Their transmission to China and Tibet differed greatly: **the Chinese transmission (Tang esoteric Buddhism)** relies chiefly on the *Mahavairocana-abhisambodhi-tantra* (garbhadhatu) and the *Vajrasekhara-tantra* (vajradhatu) and belongs largely to the yoga class and below; **the Tibetan transmission** possesses all four classes and gives weight to anuttarayoga - a difference that makes the doctrinal emphases and actual practices of Chinese and Tibetan esoteric Buddhism quite distinct.
